In this lesson we'll look at how to work with integers quickly and accurately, without always reaching for a calculator. First up, mental strategies. Three favourites are compensation, where you round one number to make it easier and then adjust, like turning 99 times 7 into 100 times 7 minus 7. Halving and doubling, where you split a tough product into friendlier numbers, like turning 15 times 16 into 30 times 8. And factoring, where you pull out a common factor, for example rewriting 24 times 15 as 12 times 30. Next, the sign rules. When you multiply or divide, two numbers with the same sign give a positive, and two with different signs give a negative. For adding and subtracting, just remember that subtracting is the same as adding the opposite. Then, always estimate first by rounding each number to one significant figure, so you can catch slips. A quick warning: when you subtract, keep the negative sign attached, so 5 minus 12 is negative 7, not 7. Now let's see it in action.